# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Windows batch files MUST materialize with CRLF line endings in every working
# tree. cmd.exe parses multi-line constructs with CR/LF assumptions; when a
# checkout delivers LF-only bytes (e.g. core.autocrlf=input), any parenthesized
# IF block aborts with:
#     ) was unexpected at this time.
# This was reproduced three times by real Windows E2E runs of the Desktop gate
# (2026-09-30). Attributes (not user git config) decide the worktree bytes, so
# the pin here overrides per-machine autocrlf/input settings.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
#
# The Linux side of the same bug class (2026-10-06):
# product/entrypoint.sh is exec'd directly by the container kernel
# (Dockerfile ENTRYPOINT), so its shebang must reach the kernel as
# "#!/bin/sh" + LF. A Windows checkout (core.autocrlf=true) materialized
# "#!/bin/sh\r" in the worktree, the owner's docker compose build baked it
# into the image, and the container died at boot with
# `exec /product/entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory` (the kernel
# looks for an interpreter named `/bin/sh\r`). eol=lf pins every *.sh
# worktree to LF on all platforms and overrides the user autocrlf
# heuristic the same way eol=crlf does for *.cmd.
*.sh text eol=lf
#
# The build context is defined by .dockerignore patterns (it keeps
# product/data/ - live site data, backups, secrets - OUT of the image).
# A trailing CR on a pattern line would make that pattern match nothing,
# so pin the file to LF in every worktree for the same reason as *.sh.
.dockerignore text eol=lf
#
# The same kernel rule applies to every other file the container execs
# directly: product/activate.py runs as `docker compose exec web
# /product/activate.py ...` from the Windows lifecycle scripts. A CRLF
# worktree (Windows core.autocrlf=true) made the kernel look for an
# interpreter named `env python3\r` - observed exit 127 in CI when the
# image was built from a simulated Windows worktree (2026-10-06), which
# also made the Activate/Deactivate scripts report "not running" on a
# machine that was actually running. Pinned like *.sh. (The other
# COPYed product scripts are interpreted by python3, never exec'd, so
# their endings are not kernel-relevant; the packaging guard still
# checks every COPY source for CR bytes.)
product/activate.py text eol=lf
# The in-image performance baseline is COPYed into the product image and is
# also executed directly by the performance qualification step. Pin it to LF
# in every Windows worktree so a local build cannot bake a CRLF shebang/script
# into the image.
product/perf_baseline.py text eol=lf
